# Chemical Engineering
#
# Balances, molecular thermodynamics, reactors, separations, process systems, and process safety as a prerequisite graph, with transport and control borrowed from their canonical engineering nodes.

#>chemistry_chemeng
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!chemistry
#:form #!curriculum
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bone

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Chemical Engineering"
 ~#claim: "Chemical engineering turns a reaction into a plant by closing mass and energy balances, sizing reactors and separations, and designing out the hazards that scale-up creates."
 ~#proof: "Felder, Fogler, Seader, and Prausnitz for the core; Linnhoff for pinch analysis; the Flixborough inquiry and the CSB Texas City report for process safety."
 ~#door: "Build the sugar-cube reactor, then read the safety cases before the unit-operation matrix."
 ~#audience: "chemical engineering students and chemists moving from flask to plant"
 continuity: "Chemical Engineering | scale-up"
 cold_open: "A reaction that behaves in a flask can run away in a tank, so chemical engineering is the craft of scale-up done safely."
}

